{
  "stage": "write",
  "item": "ch05.s12",
  "inputs_hash": "e0f5cbeaaba8ca8e7709161d994ce7bdee8afbf6c6215418b74eef7b1a19d645",
  "output_hash": "7cd08694d6c2647ae7c2a6cf5064627514d493607d74b0040aa54cd5f499a987",
  "output": {
    "section_id": "ch05.s12",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "id": "ch05.s12.p01",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p01.01",
            "text": "How does the pretrained classifier actually get adapted to the spam dataset, and why have the losses and accuracies so far not yet reflected training on this data?",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p01.02",
            "text": "Fine-tuning means implementing a training function that adjusts the parameters to minimize the training loss while also printing the validation loss and the test loss.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1401",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1403",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1404",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1407",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1409",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1411"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p01.03",
            "text": "At this point, the dataset has been downloaded and preprocessed, data loaders have been created, the model has been initialized, pretrained weights have been loaded, the model has been modified for fine-tuning, and evaluation utilities for loss and accuracy have been implemented.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1423",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1424",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1427",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1429",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1432",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1434"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p01.04",
            "text": "What has not happened yet is training on this dataset, so the parameters are not optimized for it.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1573",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1575",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1578",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1579"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p01.05",
            "text": "The next stage is to define the training loop, implement backpropagation, and use that training function to fine-tune the pretrained LLM to improve spam classification accuracy.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1438",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1440",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1442",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1444",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1582",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1583",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1585",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1587"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p01.06",
            "text": "Training is still required even when the data is supervised, such as spam and no-spam labels, so there is a training process in both pre-training and fine-tuning.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1544",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1546",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1548",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1551",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1553",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1555",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1557"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p01.07",
            "text": "The fine-tuning function is the same training function used earlier for pre-training, and it is described as very close to the earlier \"train model simple\" function.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1534",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1536",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1538",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1591",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1592",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1594",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1597"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p01.08",
            "text": "The main distinction is that this version tracks the number of text examples rather than the number of tokens.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1599",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1600",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1602",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1605"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch05.s12.p02",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p02.01",
            "text": "The loop itself is organized around epochs and batches.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p02.02",
            "text": "One epoch means going through the entire dataset once.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1449",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1452"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p02.03",
            "text": "Within each epoch, the code iterates over each batch.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1457",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1459",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1460"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p02.04",
            "text": "The first step in the code is to set the model to training mode.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1609",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1612",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1613",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1615",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1617"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p02.05",
            "text": "The second step is to reset the loss gradients from the previous batch before processing the current batch.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1619",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1621",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1624",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1626",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1630",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1633"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p02.06",
            "text": "The third and most important step is to compute the loss on the current batch, run a backward pass to calculate loss gradients, and then update the model weights with an optimizer step.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1469",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1472",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1475",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1478",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1480",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1636",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1637",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1639",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1642",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1644",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1646",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1647",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1650"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p02.07",
            "text": "After the weights are updated, the code prints the training loss and the validation loss, and this process repeats across multiple epochs.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1496",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1500",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1501",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1504",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1506"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p02.08",
            "text": "The backward pass is the most important step because it produces loss gradients with respect to the parameters, which is why the loss function must be differentiable.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1510",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1512",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1514",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1516",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1518",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1521",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1522"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p02.09",
            "text": "Once those gradients are available, the parameters can be updated repeatedly so that the loss is hopefully minimized.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1521",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1522",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1524",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1526",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1528",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1529",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1532"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch05.s12.p03",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p03.01",
            "text": "The implementation also keeps track of progress through the data and decides when to evaluate.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p03.02",
            "text": "The code tracks the number of examples seen, where \"input_batch.shape[0]\" gives the number of samples in the batch.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1678",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1680",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1681",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1684",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1687",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1692",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1694",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1702",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1704",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1706"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p03.03",
            "text": "An example seen means one text message processed by the model.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1710",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1711",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1713",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1715"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p03.04",
            "text": "Whenever the model goes through a full batch, the global step is increased by one.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1721",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1724"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p03.05",
            "text": "The evaluation frequency specifies after how many processed batches the training loss and validation loss are printed.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1729",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1733",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1734",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1765",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1767",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1769",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1772",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1776",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1778",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1781"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p03.06",
            "text": "If the training data loader has 130 batches and the evaluation frequency is 50, the training and validation loss are printed about two times per epoch.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1737",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1740",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1745",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1748",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1750",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1753",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1759",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1762",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1784",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1786",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1788",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1790",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1792",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1794",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1797",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1799"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p03.07",
            "text": "The evaluation code can compute training and validation loss on only a specified number of batches, such as five or ten, to save time.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1803",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1804",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1806",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1809",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1811",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1813",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1816",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1817",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1819",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1821",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1823",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1825",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1828",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1829",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1830"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p03.08",
            "text": "During training, the training loss and validation loss are printed after every 50 batches because of this evaluation step, and the training and validation accuracy are printed after every epoch.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1833",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1835",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1837",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1838",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1841",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1844",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1846",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1848",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1850",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1854",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1855",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1858",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1860"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch05.s12.p04",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p04.01",
            "text": "The optimizer and training call complete the fine-tuning setup.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p04.02",
            "text": "Instead of vanilla gradient descent, the optimization in practice uses a more complicated algorithm that keeps track of previous gradients and gradient squares so that optimization is done better and the model does not get stuck in local minima.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1654",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1656",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1658",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1661",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1663",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1665",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1668",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1671",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1673",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1675"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p04.03",
            "text": "\"AdamW\" is used here, and it is described as a modification of the Adam optimizer with weight decay.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1885",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1888",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1890",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1893"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p04.04",
            "text": "It is described as helping avoid local minima, converging in a smooth manner, and leading to faster convergence.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1897",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1900",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1902"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p04.05",
            "text": "The training code calls \"train classifier simple\" with the modified GPT model that has a classification head on top, together with the train loader, validation loader, optimizer, number of epochs, evaluation frequency, and evaluation iterations.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1933",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1935",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1938",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1940",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1942",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1944",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1948",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1951",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1953",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1955",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1957",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1960",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1961",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1964",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1967",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1970",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1972",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1975"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p04.06",
            "text": "In this call, the evaluation frequency is set so that training and validation loss are printed after every 50 batches, and the evaluation iterations are set to five batches to keep the calculations quick.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1975",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1978",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1979",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1981",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1983",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1986",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1988",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1991",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1992"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p04.07",
            "text": "Evaluating on only five batches is not the best evaluation method, but it still gives a useful sense of how the training loss and validation loss are progressing.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1998",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@2000",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@2002",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@2010",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@2011",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@2013"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p04.08",
            "text": "On one machine, the full training process took about 8.8 minutes, and on a typical i5, i7, or MacBook laptop it should take about 7 to 10 minutes.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1863",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1865",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1868",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1873",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1876",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1878",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@2016",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@2018",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@2020"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s12.p04.09",
            "text": "Because the optimizer choice and hyperparameters can be changed, including trying \"AdamW\" versus \"Adam\" and changing the learning rate and weight decay, this setup can be used to explore their effects on loss and accuracy.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1905",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1906",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1909",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1911",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1921",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1924",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1925",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@1928"
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "invalid_citation_sentences": 0
  }
}
